#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe16775-3759-5a79-97ca-1f38d17cc42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284127} {:answer/id #uuid "d703169c-1e19-58cf-a9d1-b6cc5d645446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284128} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ada3c1-d523-5656-bdd8-ea73b1f354ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284129} {:answer/id #uuid "5da67961-9e35-5789-b3b9-477442337571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d221da97-895b-554e-a0d0-4530a8bc7dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db89d2d-1270-5238-a5d8-8337010a987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f5f7e14-de4a-5b80-a0ab-301a0dcb6346" :question/math-academy-id "q-162199" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}]}
